===============================================================================
PART 1 : WHAT IT IS
===============================================================================

A child class replaces a method it inherited from its parent, keeping the
SAME name and the SAME parameters, but writing its own body.

    class Animal {
        void sound() { System.out.println("Some sound"); }
    }

    class Dog extends Animal {
        @Override
        void sound() { System.out.println("Bark"); }   // same name, same params
    }

WHY IT EXISTS

Overloading is for the CALLER's convenience -- one name, many input shapes.
Overriding is for EXTENSIBILITY -- it lets a child change what a method does
without the calling code being rewritten.

        void makeItSpeak(Animal a) {    // written once, never touched again
            a.sound();                  // works for Dog, Cat, Cow, and any
        }                               // animal you add next year

That method was compiled before Dog existed, yet it calls Dog's version.
That is the whole point.


===============================================================================
PART 2 : THE RULES  (all seven must be true)
===============================================================================

    1. Same method name.

    2. Same parameter list -- same types, same count, same ORDER.
       If the parameters differ it is OVERLOADING, not overriding.

    3. Inheritance must exist -- extends a class, or implements an interface.
       No IS-A relationship, no overriding.

    4. Return type must be the same, or a SUBCLASS of it (covariant).

    5. Access must be the same or WIDER. Never narrower.

    6. Checked exceptions must be the same, NARROWER, or none.
       Never new, never broader. Unchecked exceptions are unrestricted.

    7. The parent method must not be private, static or final.

Rules 4, 5 and 6 all come from one single idea -- see PART 7.


===============================================================================
PART 3 : THE FEATURES  (what it gives you, and how it behaves)
===============================================================================

    1. It is RUNTIME polymorphism. The JVM chooses the version, not the
       compiler. Also called dynamic dispatch or late binding.

    2. The OBJECT decides which version runs -- not the reference type.

    3. Only INSTANCE methods can be overridden.

    4. Variables are NEVER overridden. They are only hidden. Fields are
       resolved from the reference type at compile time.

    5. The parent version is still reachable from the child via super.method().

    6. It always needs at least TWO classes. Overloading needs only one.

    7. An abstract method MUST be overridden by the first concrete subclass.
       That is the only case where overriding is compulsory.


===============================================================================
PART 4 : @Override -- ALWAYS WRITE IT
===============================================================================

    class Child extends Parent {
        @Override
        void display() { ... }
    }

It tells the compiler: "I intend this to override a parent method." If the
signature is wrong -- a typo, a wrong parameter type, wrong capitalisation --
compilation fails immediately, instead of silently creating a brand new method
that nobody ever calls.

    class Parent { void display() { } }

    class Child extends Parent {

        void dispaly() { }      // typo. Compiles fine. NOT an override.
                                // You debug for an hour wondering why
                                // nothing happens.

        @Override
        void dispaly() { }      // same typo -> COMPILE ERROR in one second
    }

The JVM does not need it. Treat it as mandatory for yourself. Put it on every
overridden method, including the ones in ShapesExample.java and MyTest.java
that currently do not have it.


===============================================================================
PART 5 : super -- CALLING THE PARENT VERSION
===============================================================================

    class Parent {
        void show() { System.out.println("Parent show"); }
    }

    class Child extends Parent {
        @Override
        void show() {
            super.show();                       // run the parent version first
            System.out.println("Child show");   // then add extra behaviour
        }
    }

    super.method()   calls the immediate parent's version of that method
    super.variable   reads the parent's hidden field
    super(...)       calls the parent CONSTRUCTOR, must be the FIRST statement

    super cannot be used inside a static method -- there is no object there.
    super.super.method() is ILLEGAL. You can only reach up one level.

This is how you EXTEND behaviour instead of REPLACING it: do what the parent
did, then add more.


===============================================================================
PART 6 : UPCASTING AND DYNAMIC DISPATCH   (the heart of the topic)
===============================================================================

    Parent p = new Child();     // upcasting: parent reference, child object
    p.show();                   // the CHILD version runs

Two separate things are decided at two separate times:

    REFERENCE TYPE (Parent)   -- fixed at COMPILE time
        Controls WHICH METHODS YOU ARE ALLOWED TO CALL.
        p.childOnlyMethod();    // COMPILE ERROR -- not visible on Parent

    OBJECT TYPE (Child)       -- discovered at RUNTIME
        Controls WHICH VERSION ACTUALLY RUNS.
        p.show();               // Child's version runs

Memorise it as one line:

        The REFERENCE decides WHAT you can call.
        The OBJECT decides WHICH ONE runs.

The JVM performs this lookup through a virtual method table (vtable) at
runtime. That is why it is called dynamic dispatch, or late binding.

THE THREE CONDITIONS -- all must be present, miss one and you do not have it:

        1. Inheritance        (an IS-A relationship exists)
        2. Method overriding  (the child overrides the parent's method)
        3. Upcasting          (a parent reference holds a child object)

Your own MyTest.java demonstrates it:

        A a1 = new B();
        a1.m1();    // "A-m1"  -> B never overrode m1, so A's version runs
        a1.m2();    // "B-m2"  -> B overrode m2, so B's version runs
        a1.m3();    // COMPILE ERROR -> m3 is not declared in A at all

Add that third line and read the error. It is the cleanest way to feel the
difference between the reference type and the object type.


===============================================================================
PART 7 : THE THREE "NEVER SHRINK" RULES, AND THE ONE REASON BEHIND THEM
===============================================================================

Rules 4, 5 and 6 look like three things to memorise. They are one thing.

    THE PRINCIPLE
        An override is a PROMISE that the child can be used ANYWHERE the
        parent is expected. So the child may widen what it accepts and
        narrow what it demands -- but it may never break code that was
        written against the parent.

Whenever you are unsure, ask: "would this surprise somebody holding a Parent
reference?" If yes, Java forbids it.


  a) ACCESS -- same or WIDER

        private  <  default  <  protected  <  public

     Stay at the same level or move RIGHT. Moving left is a compile error.

        Parent declares:  protected void show()

        Child: public    void show()    // OK    -- moved right
        Child: protected void show()    // OK    -- same
        Child:           void show()    // ERROR -- moved left to default
        Child: private   void show()    // ERROR -- moved left

     Why: a Parent reference could otherwise call a method the object
     refuses to expose.


  b) CHECKED EXCEPTIONS -- same, NARROWER, or none

     Unchecked (RuntimeException, Error and subclasses): no restriction.
     Checked: same, or a subclass, or nothing. Never new, never broader.

        class Parent {
            void read() throws IOException { }
        }
        class Child extends Parent {
            void read()                              { }  // OK    throws none
            void read() throws IOException           { }  // OK    same
            void read() throws FileNotFoundException { }  // OK    subclass
            void read() throws Exception             { }  // ERROR broader
            void read() throws SQLException          { }  // ERROR new checked
            void read() throws RuntimeException      { }  // OK    unchecked
        }

     Why: the caller only wrote catch (IOException e). A SQLException would
     sail straight past that catch block.


  c) RETURN TYPE -- same or a SUBCLASS  (covariant, Java 5 onwards)

        class Animal {
            Animal reproduce() { return new Animal(); }
        }
        class Dog extends Animal {
            @Override
            Dog reproduce() { return new Dog(); }    // Dog IS-A Animal -> OK
        }

     The caller benefits by avoiding a cast:

            Dog puppy = new Dog().reproduce();       // no (Dog) cast needed

     Works for REFERENCE types only. Primitives must match EXACTLY -- an
     int return cannot become a long.

     Why: the caller expected an Animal. A Dog IS an Animal, so nothing
     breaks. Returning a broader type would break them.


===============================================================================
PART 8 : WHAT LOOKS LIKE OVERRIDING BUT IS NOT
===============================================================================

  a) STATIC METHODS  ->  this is METHOD HIDING

        class Parent { static void show() { System.out.println("Parent"); } }
        class Child extends Parent {
                       static void show() { System.out.println("Child");  } }

        Parent p = new Child();
        p.show();          // prints "Parent"

     Static methods belong to the CLASS, not to an object, so there is no
     dynamic dispatch. Resolved from the REFERENCE type at compile time.
     You cannot put @Override on a static method that hides another.

     Also: a child cannot turn an instance method into a static one, or a
     static one into an instance method. Both are compile errors.


  b) INSTANCE VARIABLES  ->  this is FIELD HIDING

        class Parent { String name = "Parent"; }
        class Child extends Parent { String name = "Child"; }

        Parent p = new Child();
        System.out.println(p.name);             // prints "Parent"
        System.out.println(((Child) p).name);   // prints "Child"

     VARIABLES ARE NEVER POLYMORPHIC IN JAVA. Only instance methods are.
     A favourite interview trick question.


  c) PRIVATE METHODS  ->  not inherited at all, so never overridden.
     The same signature in the child is just a new, unrelated method that
     happens to share a name.


  d) FINAL METHODS  ->  cannot be overridden. That is exactly what final is
     for: locking behaviour so no subclass can change it.


  e) CONSTRUCTORS  ->  never inherited, so never overridden. They can only
     be OVERLOADED and CHAINED (see the Constructor package).


  f) The main method  ->  it is static, so rule (a) applies. It can be
     overloaded, but never overridden.


===============================================================================
PART 9 : OVERLOADING vs OVERRIDING -- SIDE BY SIDE
===============================================================================

                          OVERLOADING              OVERRIDING
    --------------------  -----------------------  -----------------------
    Polymorphism type     Compile-time / static    Runtime / dynamic
    Who decides           The compiler             The JVM
    Decided when          Compile time             Run time
    Method name           Same                     Same
    Parameter list        MUST differ              MUST be identical
    Return type           Ignored by the compiler  Same or covariant
    Access modifier       Anything                 Same or wider
    Exceptions            Anything                 Same or narrower
    Inheritance needed    No                       Yes -- mandatory
    Number of classes     Usually one              Always two or more
    Static methods        Can be overloaded        Cannot -- that is hiding
    Private methods       Can be overloaded        Cannot -- not inherited
    Final methods         Can be overloaded        Cannot
    Purpose               Caller convenience       Extensibility

One line each:

    OVERLOADING = same name, DIFFERENT parameters, SAME class, compiler decides.
    OVERRIDING  = same name, SAME parameters, PARENT and CHILD, JVM decides.


===============================================================================
PART 10 : CHECKLIST -- IS THIS A VALID OVERRIDE?
===============================================================================

    [ ] Same method name
    [ ] Same parameter list: type, count AND order
            (if the parameters differ it is OVERLOADING, not overriding)
    [ ] IS-A relationship exists (extends a class or implements an interface)
    [ ] Return type is the same OR a subclass (covariant)
    [ ] Access modifier is the same or WIDER
    [ ] No new or broader CHECKED exception
    [ ] Parent method is not private, not static, not final
    [ ] It is an instance method
    [ ] @Override annotation is written on it


===============================================================================
PART 11 : TRAPS AND INTERVIEW QUESTIONS
===============================================================================

  1. Parent p = new Child();  which show() runs?
        The CHILD's. The object decides.

  2. Same line -- can you call a method that exists only in Child?
        No. Compile error. The reference decides what is visible.

  3. Both classes have a field called name. What does p.name print?
        The PARENT's. Fields are not polymorphic.

  4. Both show() methods are static. Which runs?
        The PARENT's. That is hiding, not overriding.

  5. Can you override a private method?
        No. It is not inherited, so there is nothing to override.

  6. Can you override main()?
        No. It is static. You can only overload it.

  7. Can the child throw a broader checked exception?
        No. Same, narrower, or none.

  8. Can the child make a public method protected?
        No. Same or wider only.

  9. Can the return type change?
        Only to a subclass, and only for reference types.

 10. Why does @Override matter if the JVM ignores it?
        It turns a silent runtime bug into an instant compile error.

 11. Changed the parameter list while trying to override -- what happened?
        You silently created an OVERLOAD. Your method never gets called.
        @Override would have caught it.


===============================================================================
PART 12 : PRACTICE LIST -- WHAT TO CODE AFTER READING THIS
===============================================================================

  1. Add @Override to every overridden method in ShapesExample.java and
     MyTest.java. Nothing should break -- that is the point.

  2. Deliberately misspell one overridden method name WITH @Override present,
     read the compiler error, then fix it.

  3. In ShapesExample, make area() actually calculate using fields
     (radius, side, length/breadth) instead of printing a formula string.

  4. Build a Shape[] array holding a Circle, a Square and a Rectangle, then
     loop over it calling area(). That single loop IS runtime polymorphism.

  5. Write a child override that calls super.show() first, then adds its own
     line, so you can see behaviour EXTENDED rather than replaced.

  6. Prove field hiding: give Parent and Child a field with the same name,
     print it through a Parent reference, then through a cast.

  7. Prove method hiding: make both show() methods static and watch the
     output flip back to the parent version.

  8. Try to override a final method and read the error message.

  9. Try to narrow access -- make a public parent method protected in the
     child -- and read that error too.
